WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.307
<v Ben & Sterling>And I started from like
the ground up where I knew

00:00:03.387 --> 00:00:08.167
<v Ben & Sterling>nothing and I was building
something to where I feel

00:00:08.247 --> 00:00:08.297
<v Ben & Sterling>like...

00:00:08.327 --> 00:00:09.417
<v Ben & Sterling>Like you literally built the machine.

00:00:09.497 --> 00:00:12.494
<v Ben & Sterling>And so I had to understand.

00:00:12.574 --> 00:00:15.954
<v Ben & Sterling>I think like people coming
into the lab in a semester

00:00:16.034 --> 00:00:18.327
<v Ben & Sterling>when things have been
built, and they're just kind

00:00:18.407 --> 00:00:21.147
<v Ben & Sterling>of like, I never got the
chance to learn any of this.

00:00:21.227 --> 00:00:25.577
<v Ben & Sterling>And then the feedback cycle can become,

00:00:25.657 --> 00:00:27.327
<v Ben & Sterling>that didn't work, try again.

00:00:27.407 --> 00:00:27.667
<v Ben & Sterling>Yeah.

00:00:27.747 --> 00:00:29.107
<v Ben & Sterling>That didn't work, try again.

00:00:29.187 --> 00:00:33.333
<v Ben & Sterling>AI could write the first draft of a paper

00:00:33.413 --> 00:00:36.980
<v Ben & Sterling>that then we read and really understand

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:40.686
<v Ben & Sterling>Xavier was explaining the
Bayesian optimization to

00:00:40.766 --> 00:00:43.046
<v Ben & Sterling>Seth and I was listening
in and I could tell that

00:00:43.126 --> 00:00:46.826
<v Ben & Sterling>Xavier really understood
that he wasn't just using

00:00:46.906 --> 00:00:47.787
<v Ben & Sterling>AI to do the learning
